      "author": "github-actions[bot]",
      "description": "Grill leans into the thing a flashcard app can't do: it works *inside your knowledge graph*.\n\n- **Find the links you're missing.** In an AI session Grill looks for two of your notes that clearly belong together but aren't linked, quizzes you on the connection, and offers a one-tap **Link these notes** button that writes the `[[link]]` into your note for you. Candidate pairs come from a cheap, offline check over your notes' vocabulary; a model call then confirms only the pairs with a real relationship, so it stays precise. On by default (AI mode only), tunable under Sessions, and the dashboard counts the connections you've made.\n- **Write your own questions.** Drop a `> [!grill]` callout into any note with your question, an optional `A:` answer, and an optional `rubric:`. Grill asks it verbatim, schedules it like everything else, and grades your typed answer against your rubric (or, without one, against the note). It's a callout, so it folds away and never clutters your prose. Existing `Question:: answer` flashcards still work exactly as before.\n- **Questions are cached now.** A generated question is saved per concept and reused on review instead of a fresh API call (and a fresh question) every time. It regenerates automatically when you edit the note; set \"Reuse generated questions\" above zero for a fresh variant now and then.\n- **Careful grading (opt-in).** Grade an answer with a small consensus of calls and keep the stricter verdict, cutting the chance of being marked correct when you weren't, at a higher per-answer cost.",
